I've tested this by changing the systemd-coredump@.service unit to belong to a temp coredump.slice which sets:

```
[Slice]
MemoryMax=100M
```

And then by killing a simple program which allocates a huge amount of memory:

```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>

main() {
  char *ptr = malloc(1024 * 1024 * 1024);
  sleep(10);
  free(ptr);
}
```

Before the change this got systemd-coredump oom-killed by the kernel, after the change it works fine. No backtrace, but the compressed core is generated successfully.

hmm, so this looks superficially OK. I have two questions though:

This patch assumes that the cgroup memory limits apply to files that processes write to tmpfs. Is this really correct? @htejun is this true, are writes to tmpfs charged against memory use by memcg?

@htejun this patch adds a new D-Bus property MemoryAvailable that returns the effective "available" memory for a cgroup, which is defined done by subtracting the MemoryUsage value of each unit from the MemoryMax value, starting from the relevant cgroup, going up the tree for each slice. Does this make sense at all? Should this use MemoryHigh instead?

